    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "006162da_dff84296",
        "filename": "Documentation/dev-contributing.txt",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 165,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T15:33:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Do we actually require DCO sign-off for Gerrit contributions?\n\nNo, we don\u0027t require `Signed-off-by` on each commit. However, we require the authorship declaration, and it *must* match the Google CLA the contributor has signed to be allowed to make changes.\n\nFrom the Google CLA:\n- _\"5. You represent that each of Your Contributions is Your original creation _\"\n- _\"7. Should You wish to submit work that is not Your original creation, You may submit it to Google separately from any Contribution\"_\n\nI am not a lawyer; however, I don\u0027t believe that the lack of a `Signed-off-by` footer in the commit would nullify the Google CLA points 5 and 7 that *we signed off* as individual or company contributors.\n\nAFAIK the fact that the contribution *must* be original creation by the author  isn\u0027t new and therefore should not go through any additional process.\n\nI am happy to rephrase it in terms of points 5. and 7. of the Google CLA rather than mentioning the DCO.",
      "parentUuid": "01dd55ea_cc569655",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 165,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 165,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "d936dee47d0f001cea35abca1c9e5ba78a76d543",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "250e87b4_74348a17",
        "filename": "Documentation/dev-contributing.txt",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 170,
      "author": {
        "id": 1072905
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T07:31:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How would contributors realistically certify that the AI tools they use don\u0027t infringe any third-party copyrights?\n\nThey can review the generated output and check for known licensing issues, but they can\u0027t verify the model\u0027s training data or guarantee that no protected material was reproduced.\n\nI think we should frame this as \"due diligence\", ensure they have the rights to submit the generated code, and disclose any known third-party licensing restrictions. This is also what the Google CLA actually requires in Section 5. [1]\n\n```\nYou represent that Your Contribution submissions include complete details of any third-party license or \nother restriction (including, but not limited to, related patents and trademarks) of which you are \npersonally aware and which are associated with any part of Your Contributions.\n```\n\n[1] https://cla.developers.google.com/about/google-individual",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 170,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 170,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "d936dee47d0f001cea35abca1c9e5ba78a76d543",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
